{"task_id": "Mbpp/160", "solution": "def extended_gcd(a, b):\n    \"\"\"\n    Implements the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find gcd(a, b) and coefficients x, y.\n    Returns (g, x, y) such that a*x + b*y = g = gcd(a, b).\n    \"\"\"\n    if a == 0 and b == 0:\n        return (0, 0, 0)\n\n    if b == 0:\n        # Base case: gcd(a, 0) is |a|. x_g is 1 if a >= 0, -1 if a < 0; y_g is 0.\n        return (abs(a), 1 if a >= 0 else -1, 0)\n\n    # Recursive step\n    gcd_val, x_prev, y_prev = extended_gcd(b, a % b)\n\n    # Update x_g and y_g using the results from the recursive call\n    x_g = y_prev\n    y_g = x_prev - (a // b) * y_prev\n\n    return (gcd_val, x_g, y_g)\ndef find_solution(a, b, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds an integer solution (x, y) for the linear Diophantine equation ax + by = n.\n    Returns (x, y) if a solution exists, otherwise None.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Special case: a=0 and b=0\n    if a == 0 and b == 0:\n        if n == 0:\n            return (0, 0)  # 0x + 0y = 0 has infinite solutions, (0,0) is one.\n        else:\n            return None    # 0x + 0y = n (non-zero) has no solution.\n\n    # Calculate gcd(a, b) and B\u00e9zout coefficients x_g, y_g\n    gcd_val, x_g, y_g = extended_gcd(a, b)\n\n    # If n is not divisible by gcd_val, no integer solution exists.\n    if gcd_val == 0: # This case specifically handles gcd(0,X)=X and gcd(X,0)=X etc.\n        # But if gcd_val is truly 0, it means a and b were both 0, which is handled above.\n        # So this `if` is mostly a safeguard, though `n % 0` would raise an error.\n        # However, the extended_gcd should return abs(a) or abs(b) if one is zero,\n        # so gcd_val will only be 0 if a and b were initially both 0.\n        # The previous check `if a == 0 and b == 0:` already covers this.\n        if n != 0:\n            return None # Should not be reachable if (0,0) is handled correctly.\n    elif n % gcd_val != 0:\n        return None\n\n    # Calculate the scaling factor k and a particular solution (x, y).\n    k = n // gcd_val\n    x = x_g * k\n    y = y_g * k\n\n    return (x, y)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/161", "solution": "def remove_elements(original_list: list, elements_to_remove: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Removes elements from original_list that are present in elements_to_remove, returning a new list.\n    This function optimizes lookup performance by converting the elements to remove into a set.\n    \"\"\"\n    elements_to_remove_set = set(elements_to_remove)\n    new_list = []\n    for element in original_list:\n        if element not in elements_to_remove_set:\n            new_list.append(element)\n    return new_list"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/162", "solution": "def sum_series(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of the series (n - 2*i) for i from 0 to n // 2.\n    Adds each term to a running total before returning the final sum.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    # Iterate 'i' from 0 up to and including n // 2\n    for i in range((n // 2) + 1):\n        term = n - (2 * i)\n        total_sum += term\n    return total_sum"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/161", "solution": "from typing import Any\ndef remove_elements(source_list: list[Any], elements_to_remove: list[Any]) -> list[Any]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Filters a source list by removing elements present in a specified removal list.\n    Preserves the relative order of remaining elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    elements_to_remove_set = set(elements_to_remove)\n    resultant_list = []\n    for element in source_list:\n        if element not in elements_to_remove_set:\n            resultant_list.append(element)\n    return resultant_list"}
