{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total number of sequences of length n, where each element x_i is a positive integer,\n    satisfies x_i >= 2 * x_{i-1}, and x_i <= m.\n    \"\"\"\n    memo = {}\n\n    def _count_sequences_recursive(current_length, last_element_value):\n        \"\"\"\n        Recursively counts sequences from the current state, using memoization to store results.\n        It returns the number of valid sequences that can be completed from this state.\n        \"\"\"\n        if (current_length, last_element_value) in memo:\n            return memo[(current_length, last_element_value)]\n\n        # Base Case 1: Sequence is complete, meaning a valid sequence of length n was formed.\n        if current_length == n:\n            return 1\n\n        sub_count = 0\n        # Determine the starting value for the next element based on the constraint x_i >= 2 * x_{i-1}.\n        start_next = 2 * last_element_value\n\n        # Iterate through possible values for the next element, respecting x_i <= m.\n        for next_element in range(start_next, m + 1):\n            sub_count += _count_sequences_recursive(current_length + 1, next_element)\n\n        memo[(current_length, last_element_value)] = sub_count\n        return sub_count\n\n    total_sequences = 0\n    # The first element x_1 must satisfy x_1 * (2^(n-1)) <= m to allow for a sequence of length n.\n    # Therefore, x_1 <= m / (2^(n-1)).\n    # We use max_x1_divisor to prevent potential overflow issues with very large n if 2**(n-1) is computed directly.\n    # However, Python handles large integers, so this is primarily for conceptual clarity.\n    # In practice, if n-1 is very large, m // (2**(n-1)) would become 0 quickly.\n\n    # Calculate 2^(n-1) avoiding large powers if n-1 is huge by checking against m early.\n    # The smallest possible value for x_1 is 1.\n    # The sequence would be x_1, 2*x_1, 4*x_1, ..., x_1 * 2^(n-1)\n    # The last element x_n must be <= m. So, x_1 * 2^(n-1) <= m.\n    # This implies x_1 <= m / (2^(n-1)).\n\n    # Check if a sequence of length n is even possible. If 1 * 2^(n-1) > m, no sequence can be formed.\n    # This also helps bound the iteration for x1.\n\n    min_last_element_val_for_x1 = 1\n    for _ in range(n - 1):\n        # This loop calculates the minimum possible value for the last element in a sequence\n        # if the first element was 1. If this minimum exceeds m, then no sequence is possible.\n        if min_last_element_val_for_x1 > m // 2: # Avoids overflow for min_last_element_val_for_x1 * 2 if m is large\n            min_last_element_val_for_x1 = m + 1 # Set to value > m to effectively break condition\n            break\n        min_last_element_val_for_x1 *= 2\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0 # Or 1 if an empty sequence is considered valid, but problem states positive integers and length n.\n    if n == 1:\n        return m # Any number from 1 to m is a valid sequence of length 1.\n\n    # Iteration for the first element x_1.\n    # The first element must be at least 1.\n    # The first element x_1 cannot be greater than m.\n    # Also, x_1 * (2^(n-1)) must be less than or equal to m.\n    # So, x_1 <= m / (2^(n-1)).\n\n    # Calculate the maximum possible value for the first element.\n    # If 2**(n-1) is very large, max_x1_for_sequence_end will be 0.\n    max_x1_for_sequence_end = m // (2**(n-1)) if (n-1) >= 0 else m\n\n    for x1 in range(1, min(m, max_x1_for_sequence_end) + 1):\n        total_sequences += _count_sequences_recursive(1, x1)\n\n    return total_sequences"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def _count_sequences_recursive(current_length: int, last_element_value: int, m: int, n: int, memo: dict) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Recursively counts valid sequences using memoization.\"\"\"\n    if current_length == n:\n        return 1\n\n    memo_key = (current_length, last_element_value)\n    if memo_key in memo:\n        return memo[memo_key]\n\n    count = 0\n    # Iterate for the next element, ensuring it's at least twice the last element and <= m.\n    for next_element in range(last_element_value * 2, m + 1):\n        count += _count_sequences_recursive(current_length + 1, next_element, m, n, memo)\n\n    memo[memo_key] = count\n    return count\ndef get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total number of possible sequences of length n.\n    Each element must be a positive integer, >= twice the previous, and <= m.\n    \"\"\"\n    _validate_inputs(m, n)\n\n    memo = {}\n    total_count = 0\n\n    # Iterate through all possible first elements for the sequence.\n    for first_element in range(1, m + 1):\n        total_count += _count_sequences_recursive(1, first_element, m, n, memo)\n\n    return total_count"}
