{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "def max_sum(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence in the given array.\n    A bitonic subsequence is first increasing and then decreasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    # Handle edge cases for empty or single-element arrays.\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return arr[0]\n\n    # Calculate Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) sums ending at each element.\n    lis_sums = list(arr)\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lis_sums[i] = max(lis_sums[i], lis_sums[j] + arr[i])\n\n    # Calculate Longest Decreasing Subsequence (LDS) sums starting at each element.\n    lds_sums = list(arr)\n    for i in range(n - 2, -1, -1):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lds_sums[i] = max(lds_sums[i], lds_sums[j] + arr[i])\n\n    # Combine LIS and LDS sums, adjusting for double-counted peak, and find maximum.\n    max_bitonic_sum = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        # A true bitonic sequence requires both an increasing and a decreasing phase.\n        # This condition ensures that the peak element isn't considered as a bitonic sequence\n        # if it's merely part of an increasing or decreasing sequence, but not both.\n        # For a valid bitonic subsequence, there must be at least one element smaller than arr[i] to its left\n        # and at least one element smaller than arr[i] to its right, that could form the LIS and LDS.\n        # A simplified check: if lis_sums[i] > arr[i] and lds_sums[i] > arr[i], it implies\n        # that there was an increasing sequence ending at arr[i] and a decreasing sequence starting at arr[i],\n        # where arr[i] is a peak for both.\n        # However, a more robust check should consider cases where the bitonic sequence is\n        # strictly increasing followed by strictly decreasing, meaning at least two elements involved.\n        # The LIS and LDS sums will be greater than arr[i] if there's at least one preceding/succeeding element\n        # that extends the sum.\n        current_bitonic_sum = lis_sums[i] + lds_sums[i] - arr[i]\n        max_bitonic_sum = max(max_bitonic_sum, current_bitonic_sum)\n\n    # If no bitonic sequence was found (e.g., strictly increasing or strictly decreasing array),\n    # the max_bitonic_sum would still be 0.\n    # The problem implies we are looking for a sequence that is first increasing AND THEN decreasing.\n    # If the array is e.g. [1, 2, 3], LIS sum at 3 is 6, LDS sum at 3 is 3.\n    # current_bitonic_sum = 6 + 3 - 3 = 6. This is the sum of [1,2,3].\n    # But [1,2,3] is not bitonic.\n    # A bitonic sequence must have a peak.\n    # The initial value for max_bitonic_sum should be able to handle arrays like [10, 20, 5, 4]\n    # In this case, the `max_bitonic_sum` initialized to 0 would correctly find 35.\n    # For a strictly increasing sequence, the LDS for the last element will be just the element itself.\n    # For a strictly decreasing sequence, the LIS for the first element will be just the element itself.\n    # The current logic will correctly find the maximum sum.\n    # For example, [1, 2, 3]. lis_sums = [1, 3, 6], lds_sums = [1, 2, 3].\n    # i=0: arr[0]=1. lis_sums[0]=1, lds_sums[0]=1. sum = 1+1-1=1. max_bitonic_sum = 1.\n    # i=1: arr[1]=2. lis_sums[1]=3, lds_sums[1]=2. sum = 3+2-2=3. max_bitonic_sum = 3.\n    # i=2: arr[2]=3. lis_sums[2]=6, lds_sums[2]=3. sum = 6+3-3=6. max_bitonic_sum = 6.\n    # This is correct because the longest increasing subsequence is [1,2,3] with sum 6.\n    # A bitonic subsequence can degenerate to a strictly increasing or strictly decreasing sequence.\n    # If the problem strictly means \"first increasing AND THEN decreasing\" with a change in direction,\n    # then the condition for a peak must be more specific.\n    # The prompt \"where a sequence is bitonic if it is first increasing and then decreasing\" implies\n    # that it can be strictly increasing (decreasing part is empty) or strictly decreasing (increasing part is empty).\n    # However, standard definition of bitonic sequence often implies a \"peak\" where the sequence increases up to it,\n    # and decreases from it.\n    # Given the example `max_sum([1, 15, 51, 45, 33, 100, 12, 18, 9]) == 194`,\n    # The bitonic sequence is [1, 15, 51, 100, 18, 9] summing to 194.\n    # Here, 100 is the peak.\n    # The current code implements a standard approach where `lis_sums[i]` and `lds_sums[i]` are\n    # the maximum sum of increasing/decreasing subsequences ending/starting at `arr[i]`.\n    # The \"minus arr[i]\" correctly adjusts for `arr[i]` being part of both.\n    # If an array is strictly increasing, say [1,2,3], then lis_sums = [1,3,6] and lds_sums = [1,2,3].\n    # For i=2, arr[2]=3, current_bitonic_sum = 6+3-3=6.\n    # For a strictly decreasing array, say [3,2,1], then lis_sums = [3,2,1] and lds_sums = [3,5,6].\n    # For i=0, arr[0]=3, current_bitonic_sum = 3+3-3=3.\n    # The final `max_bitonic_sum` correctly captures the sum of the LIS/LDS if no \"true\" bitonic sequence exists.\n    # The current implementation correctly handles the definition of a bitonic sequence as\n    # \"first increasing and then decreasing\", allowing for either part to be empty.\n    # For the problem statement, the current solution seems robust.\n    return max_bitonic_sum"}
