{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Sets the leftmost '0' bit in the binary representation of n.\n    It iterates from bit 0 upwards to find the first unset bit within 64 bits.\n    \"\"\"\n    for i in range(64):  # Iterate through bit positions from 0 to 63\n        if (n & (1 << i)) == 0:  # Check if the i-th bit is unset\n            return n | (1 << i)  # Set the i-th bit and return\n\n    # If all bits from 0 to 63 are already set, set the 64th bit\n    return n | (1 << 64)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Identifies and sets the most significant (left-most) unset bit within a given integer.\n    Returns the new integer with the specified bit set.\"\"\"\n\n    # Handle the case where n is 0. The left-most unset bit is at position 0.\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Get the number of bits required to represent n.\n    # For n=10 (1010), bit_length() is 4. The bits are at positions 3, 2, 1, 0.\n    num_bits = n.bit_length()\n\n    # Iterate from the most significant bit (n.bit_length() - 1) down to 0.\n    for i in range(num_bits - 1, -1, -1):\n        # Check if the i-th bit is unset (0).\n        # (n & (1 << i)) will be 0 if the bit is unset, non-zero if set.\n        if not (n & (1 << i)):\n            # If unset, set it by performing a bitwise OR with a mask for that position.\n            return n | (1 << i)\n\n    # If the loop completes, all bits from num_bits - 1 down to 0 were already set.\n    # This means n is of the form 2^k - 1 (e.g., 7, 15).\n    # In this case, the left-most unset bit is at position num_bits.\n    return n | (1 << num_bits)"}
